[Event "Donald Byrne – Fischer, New York 1956 (the Game of the Century)"]
[White "Donald Byrne"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Date "1956.??.??"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Punish slow development. Coordinated pieces beat a lone queen. Fischer was 13. White's
queen moves again and again (Qb3, Qxc4, Qc5) while his king stays in the centre. Watch how
every Black move brings a new piece into play.} 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. d4 O-O
5. Bf4 d5 6. Qb3 dxc4 {Fischer gives up his centre pawn to gain time. White's queen has to
move again.} 7. Qxc4 c6 8. e4 Nbd7 9. Rd1 Nb6 10. Qc5 Bg4 {Develops with a pin on the f3
knight, which guards d4. White's queen is about to move for the fourth time.} 11. Bg5 Na4
{Question: White's king is still on e1 and his queen is loose on c5. Can you strike at
once? A shock. The knight goes where a knight can take it. After 12.Nxa4 Nxe4, Black hits
the queen on c5 and the bishop on g5, and the g7 bishop wakes up against d4. Byrne
declined with 12.Qa3, but after 12...Nxc3 13.bxc3 Nxe4 Black had won a pawn and torn the
centre open.} 12. Qa3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Nxe4 14. Bxe7 Qb6 {White's bishop attacks the rook on
f8. Fischer ignores it and hits f2 instead. While White's king sits in the centre, one
rook doesn't matter.} 15. Bc4 Nxc3 16. Bc5 Rfe8+ 17. Kf1 Be6 {Question: Your queen on b6
is attacked. Will you save it? The move of the century. Fischer leaves the queen en prise.
After 18.Bxb6 Bxc4+ comes a 'windmill' of discovered checks: 19.Kg1 Ne2+ 20.Kf1 Nxd4+
21.Kg1 Ne2+ 22.Kf1 Nc3+ 23.Kg1 axb6. Black collects a bishop and a pawn, and the rook on
d1 falls next. The engine confirms 17...Be6 is the best move by far.} 18. Bxb6 Bxc4+ 19.
Kg1 Ne2+ 20. Kf1 Nxd4+ 21. Kg1 Ne2+ 22. Kf1 Nc3+ 23. Kg1 axb6 {Take stock. Black has rook,
two bishops and knight working together. White has a queen with no help.} 24. Qb4 Ra4 25.
Qxb6 Nxd1 26. h3 Rxa2 {Fischer mops up with no risk. His king is completely safe.} 27. Kh2
Nxf2 28. Re1 Rxe1 29. Qd8+ Bf8 30. Nxe1 Bd5 31. Nf3 Ne4 32. Qb8 b5 33. h4 h5 34. Ne5 Kg7
35. Kg1 Bc5+ 36. Kf1 Ng3+ 37. Ke1 Bb4+ 38. Kd1 Bb3+ 39. Kc1 Ne2+ 40. Kb1 Nc3+ 41. Kc1 Rc2#
{Mate with rook, bishop and knight. White's queen is still on the board, a spectator.} 0-1

[Event "Fischer – Fine, New York 1963 (Evans Gambit)"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Fine"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{In the Italian, f7 and the e-file are the targets. Don't grab pawns with your king in the
centre. The Evans Gambit is an Italian Game where White gives a pawn for time. This is
your opening family, so watch how fast it goes wrong for Black when he takes material and
delays castling.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 {The gambit. If Black takes, c3 and
d4 come with tempo against the bishop.} 4... Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 exd4 7. O-O {A second
pawn gone. White already has three pieces aiming at the black king. Black has none out.}
7... dxc3 8. Qb3 {Question: What are the two weakest points in Black's camp? Queen and
bishop both point at f7, Black's weakest square. Black has to spend another move defending
it.} 8... Qe7 9. Nxc3 Nf6 10. Nd5 {Every move comes with a threat. The knight hits e7 and
c7.} 10... Nxd5 11. exd5 Ne5 12. Nxe5 Qxe5 13. Bb2 {Count development. White has queen,
two bishops and both rooks ready. Black has only his queen out.} 13... Qg5 14. h4 Qxh4 15.
Bxg7 {The bishop takes g7 and hits the rook. The e-file is about to open with check.}
15... Rg8 16. Rfe1+ Kd8 17. Qg3 {Question: Black's king is stuck on d8. Find the quiet
queen move that ends the game. Black resigned. The engine finds mate in 8. The black king
never got off the e- and d-files while White's pieces all point at it.} 1-0

[Event "Fischer – Benko, US Championship 1963 (19.Rf6!!)"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Benko"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Open lines toward the king, bring every piece, and look for quiet killers. Against the
Pirc, Fischer grabs space with f4, opens the f-file, and finds one of the most famous
quiet moves ever played.} 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 {The Austrian Attack. White
takes space and prepares e5 or f5.} 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nc6
9. Be3 e5 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. f5 {Opens the f-file against the castled king. After 11...gxf5
12.Qxf5, Black's kingside is full of holes.} 11... gxf5 12. Qxf5 Nd4 13. Qf2 Ne8 14. O-O
Nd6 15. Qg3 Kh8 16. Qg4 c6 17. Qh5 Qe8 18. Bxd4 {Fischer gives up a bishop to remove
Black's best piece, the d4 knight. The d-pawn that recaptures blocks Black's own g7
bishop.} 18... exd4 19. Rf6 {Question: No captures, no checks. Find a quiet move that adds
a piece to the attack. Black can't take. 19...Bxf6 20.e5 opens the Bd3 against h7, and
Qxh7 is mate. The rook also threatens Rxd6 and Rh6. The engine rates it about +7. Benko
played 19...Kg8 20.e5 h6 21.Ne2, and resigned.} 19... Kg8 20. e5 h6 21. Ne2 {The last
piece joins (Nf4 next). Black resigned.} 1-0

[Event "Robert Byrne – Fischer, US Championship 1963"]
[White "Robert Byrne"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Date "1963.??.??"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Calculate forcing lines (checks, captures, threats) all the way to the end. A
Grünfeld-style position with a quiet look. Fischer had seen far deeper than anyone in the
room.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 c6 4. Bg2 d5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. e3 O-O 8. Nge2 Nc6
9. O-O b6 10. b3 Ba6 11. Ba3 Re8 12. Qd2 e5 {A central break while White's queen and rook
stand awkwardly on the d-file.} 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Rfd1 Nd3 {The knight lands in a hole in
White's camp.} 15. Qc2 Nxf2 {Question: Your knights are close to White's king. Is there a
breakthrough? A deep combination. 16.Kxf2 Ng4+ 17.Kg1 Nxe3 wins the e3 pawn and hits queen
and rook. 18...Nxg2 removes the king's defender, and 19...d4! opens the long diagonal.
Grandmasters commenting live thought Fischer was lost. Byrne resigned when he saw the
mating net.} 16. Kxf2 Ng4+ 17. Kg1 Nxe3 18. Qd2 Nxg2 19. Kxg2 d4 {Opens the long diagonal
from b7 to g2 for the bishop, and frees the g7 bishop.} 20. Nxd4 Bb7+ 21. Kf1 Qd7 {Quiet
and decisive. ...Qh3+ is coming with mate. White resigned.} 0-1

[Event "Fischer – Myagmarsuren, Sousse 1967 (King's Indian Attack)"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Myagmarsuren"]
[Date "1967.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Let the pawn structure choose your plan. Attack where your pawns give you space. You
sometimes play Réti / King's Indian Attack set-ups. This is the model game for them: e5
for space, then pieces and pawns pour onto the kingside.} 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4.
g3 c5 5. Bg2 Nc6 6. Ngf3 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. e5 {The key move. The e5 pawn kicks the f6
knight, so Black's kingside has fewer defenders, and it points the way: attack on the
kingside.} 8... Nd7 9. Re1 b5 10. Nf1 b4 11. h4 {Pawn storm. Black is racing on the
queenside, so speed matters.} 11... a5 12. Bf4 a4 13. a3 bxa3 14. bxa3 Na5 15. Ne3 {The
typical reroute Nd2-f1-e3. From here the knight can go to g4 or g5.} 15... Ba6 16. Bh3 d4
17. Nf1 Nb6 18. Ng5 Nd5 19. Bd2 Bxg5 20. Bxg5 Qd7 21. Qh5 {Queen, bishop and knight now
aim at the black king.} 21... Rfc8 22. Nd2 Nc3 23. Bf6 {Question: Can you put a piece
where Black can't get rid of it? Black can only remove the bishop with ...gxf6, which
opens his own king. It cuts the king off and supports Qg7 ideas. The engine agrees this is
best.} 23... Qe8 24. Ne4 g6 25. Qg5 Nxe4 26. Rxe4 c4 27. h5 {The h-file opens with the
rook already behind the pawn.} 27... cxd3 28. Rh4 Ra7 29. Bg2 dxc2 30. Qh6 Qf8 31. Qxh7+
{Question: Find the forced mate. 31...Kxh7 32.hxg6+ (discovered check from the h4 rook)
Kxg6 33.Be4#. A queen sacrifice that mates.} 1-0

[Event "Fischer – Taimanov, Candidates match 1971, game 2"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Taimanov"]
[Date "1971.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Bishop vs knight endgames. Trade into the endgame you know is winning. Fischer won this
match 6-0. The game ran 89 moves. You start guessing at move 70, in a rook and minor-piece
endgame. Watch how Fischer chooses which pieces to trade.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4
4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nb5 d6 6. Bf4 e5 7. Be3 Nf6 8. Bg5 Qa5+ 9. Qd2 Nxe4 10. Qxa5 Nxa5 11. Be3
Kd7 12. N1c3 Nxc3 13. Nxc3 Kd8 14. Nb5 Be6 15. O-O-O b6 16. f4 exf4 17. Bxf4 Nb7 18. Be2
Bd7 19. Rd2 Be7 20. Rhd1 Bxb5 21. Bxb5 Kc7 22. Re2 Bf6 23. Rde1 Rac8 24. Bc4 Rhf8 25. b4
a5 26. Bd5 Kb8 27. a3 Rfd8 28. Bxf7 Bc3 29. Bd2 d5 30. Rd1 d4 31. Bxc3 Rxc3 32. Kb2 d3 33.
Kxc3 dxe2 34. Re1 Nd6 35. Bh5 Nb5+ 36. Kb2 axb4 37. axb4 Rd4 38. c3 Rh4 39. Bxe2 Nd6 40.
Rd1 Kc7 41. h3 Rf4 42. Rf1 Re4 43. Bd3 Re5 44. Rf2 h5 45. c4 Rg5 46. Kc3 Kd7 47. Ra2 Kc8
48. Kd4 Kc7 49. Ra7+ Kd8 50. c5 bxc5+ 51. bxc5 Ne8 52. Ra2 Nc7 53. Bc4 Kd7 54. Rb2 Kc6 55.
Bb3 Nb5+ 56. Ke3 Kxc5 57. Kf4 Rg6 58. Bd1 h4 59. Kf5 Rh6 60. Kg5 Nd6 61. Bc2 Nf7+ 62. Kg4
Ne5+ 63. Kf4 Kd4 64. Rb4+ Kc3 65. Rb5 Nf7 66. Rc5+ Kd4 67. Rf5 g5+ 68. Kg4 Ne5+ 69. Kxg5
Rg6+ 70. Kxh4 Rxg2 71. Bd1 Rg8 72. Bg4 Ke4 73. Kg3 Rg7 74. Rf4+ Kd5 75. Ra4 Ng6 76. Ra6
Ne5 77. Kf4 Rf7+ 78. Kg5 Rg7+ 79. Kf5 Rf7+ 80. Rf6 {Question: Would you trade rooks here?
Most players keep the rooks when they're a pawn up. Fischer saw that the bishop and passed
h-pawn beat the knight. The bishop can guard one side of the board and stop the knight at
long range, while the white king walks forward. A knight can't cover both wings.} 80...
Rxf6+ 81. Kxf6 Ke4 82. Bc8 Kf4 83. h4 {The passed pawn runs. The bishop covers the squares
the knight needs.} 83... Nf3 84. h5 Ng5 85. Bf5 {Domination. The knight has to shuttle
between f3 and g5 and can't stop both the pawn and the king.} 85... Nf3 86. h6 Ng5 87. Kg6
Nf3 88. h7 Ne5+ 89. Kf6 {Black resigned. The h-pawn queens. Bishop vs knight with pawns on
both wings, or with a fast passed pawn, usually favours the bishop.} 1-0

[Event "Fischer – Petrosian, Candidates final 1971, game 7"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Petrosian"]
[Date "1971.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Rules are guidelines. Activity of rooks and king decides endgames. Against the Sicilian
(a Kan with 5.Bd3), Fischer heads for an endgame with an isolated black d-pawn, then
breaks a famous textbook rule.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. Bd3 Nc6 6.
Nxc6 bxc6 7. O-O d5 8. c4 {Strikes Black's pawn centre at once.} 8... Nf6 9. cxd5 cxd5 10.
exd5 exd5 11. Nc3 Be7 12. Qa4+ {Forces the queens off. Fischer is happy in an endgame
where Black's d5 pawn is isolated.} 12... Qd7 13. Re1 Qxa4 14. Nxa4 Be6 15. Be3 O-O 16.
Bc5 {Trades off Black's good bishop. Black keeps the light bishop, which is hemmed in by
his own d5 pawn.} 16... Rfe8 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. b4 Kf8 19. Nc5 Bc8 20. f3 Rea7 21. Re5 Bd7
22. Nxd7+ {Question: Would you trade your excellent knight for Black's bad bishop? This
breaks the 'good knight vs bad bishop' rule, but Fischer calculated it. After the trade
his rook reaches c7 at once, and Black's knight can't match the active white rooks and
king. The engine rates both this and a4 as clearly better for White.} 22... Rxd7 23. Rc1
Rd6 24. Rc7 {Rook on the seventh rank, attacking pawns from behind.} 24... Nd7 25. Re2 g6
26. Kf2 h5 27. f4 h4 28. Kf3 f5 29. Ke3 d4+ 30. Kd2 Nb6 31. Ree7 {Two rooks on the
seventh. The black king is boxed in.} 31... Nd5 32. Rf7+ Ke8 33. Rb7 Nxb4 34. Bc4 {Black
resigned. Fischer won the match and went on to play Spassky.} 1-0

[Event "Fischer – Spassky, World Championship 1972, game 6"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Spassky"]
[Date "1972.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Create a weakness, fix it, then open a second front. Fischer, a lifelong 1.e4 player,
opened with 1.c4 for a surprise. A pure positional masterpiece. Spassky applauded at the
end.} 1. c4 e6 2. Nf3 d5 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bg5 O-O 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5
9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Be6 12. Qa4 c5 13. Qa3 Rc8 14. Bb5 {Pressure on Black's
hanging pawns on c5 and d5.} 14... a6 15. dxc5 bxc5 16. O-O Ra7 17. Be2 Nd7 18. Nd4 Qf8
19. Nxe6 {Knight for bishop. Black is left with a weak e6 pawn and a fixed centre.} 19...
fxe6 20. e4 {Question: Black's centre pawns stand on c5 and d5. How do you break them?
Strikes the centre at once. 20...d4 gives Black a protected passed pawn, but it is blocked
and harmless, and White gets f4-f5 and the e5 square.} 20... d4 21. f4 Qe7 22. e5 Rb8 23.
Bc4 Kh8 24. Qh3 Nf8 25. b3 a5 26. f5 {The f-pawn break opens the f-file toward the black
king.} 26... exf5 27. Rxf5 Nh7 28. Rcf1 Qd8 29. Qg3 Re7 30. h4 Rbb7 31. e6 {A passed pawn
on the sixth rank cramps Black's whole position.} 31... Rbc7 32. Qe5 Qe8 33. a4 Qd8 34.
R1f2 Qe8 35. R2f3 Qd8 36. Bd3 Qe8 37. Qe4 Nf6 38. Rxf6 {Question: Black's only good
defender is the f6 knight. What would you do? The exchange sacrifice removes it. The
engine says White is winning anyway, but this is the clearest way.} 38... gxf6 39. Rxf6
Kg8 40. Bc4 Kh8 41. Qf4 {Spassky resigned and joined the audience's applause.} 1-0

[Event "Fischer – Tal, Bled 1961 (beating the Sicilian)"]
[White "Fischer"]
[Black "Tal"]
[Date "1961.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Against the Sicilian: develop fast, hit early queen moves, open lines while the king is
in the centre. You lose most often to the Sicilian as White. Here Fischer beats Tal, the
great attacker, at his own game.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6.
g3 Nf6 7. Ndb5 {Hits the queen and eyes d6. Black's early queen move costs time.} 7... Qb8
8. Bf4 Ne5 9. Be2 Bc5 10. Bxe5 {Trades a bishop for a knight to drag the queen onto e5,
where White's pawns can hit it.} 10... Qxe5 11. f4 Qb8 12. e5 {Question: Black's king is
in the centre and his queen is far away. Can you open lines? Fischer lets the b5 knight
go. After 12...a6 13.exf6 axb5 14.fxg7 Rg8, Black's kingside is wrecked and his king will
never be safe. The engine likes it (about +3).} 12... a6 13. exf6 axb5 14. fxg7 Rg8 15.
Ne4 Be7 16. Qd4 Ra4 17. Nf6+ {Forces off Black's bishop. The dark squares around Black's
king are now White's.} 17... Bxf6 18. Qxf6 Qc7 19. O-O-O Rxa2 20. Kb1 Ra6 21. Bxb5 Rb6 22.
Bd3 e5 23. fxe5 {Fischer lets his queen go for a rook, because the pawns on f6 and g7 are
monsters.} 23... Rxf6 24. exf6 Qc5 25. Bxh7 Qg5 26. Bxg8 Qxf6 27. Rhf1 Qxg7 28. Bxf7+ Kd8
29. Be6 Qh6 30. Bxd7 Bxd7 31. Rf7 Qxh2 32. Rdxd7+ Ke8 33. Rde7+ Kd8 34. Rd7+ Kc8 35. Rc7+
Kd8 36. Rfd7+ Ke8 37. Rd1 b5 38. Rb7 Qh5 39. g4 Qh3 40. g5 Qf3 41. Re1+ Kf8 42. Rxb5 Kg7
43. Rb6 Qg3 44. Rd1 Qc7 45. Rdd6 Qc8 46. b3 Kh7 47. Ra6 1-0

[Event "Spassky – Fischer, World Championship 28th 1972, game 3"]
[White "Spassky"]
[Black "Fischer"]
[Date "1972.??.??"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Fight back after a bad start, with an unbalanced position and initiative. World
Championship 1972, game 3. Fischer's first-ever win against Spassky, after losing game 1
and forfeiting game 2. A Modern Benoni with the bold ...Nh5.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5
4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nd2 Nbd7 8. e4 Bg7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. Qc2 Nh5
12. Bxh5 gxh5 13. Nc4 Ne5 14. Ne3 Qh4 15. Bd2 Ng4 16. Nxg4 hxg4 17. Bf4 Qf6 18. g3 Bd7 19.
a4 b6 20. Rfe1 a6 21. Re2 b5 22. Rae1 Qg6 23. b3 Re7 24. Qd3 Rb8 25. axb5 axb5 26. b4 c4
27. Qd2 Rbe8 28. Re3 h5 29. R3e2 Kh7 30. Re3 Kg8 31. R3e2 Bxc3 32. Qxc3 Rxe4 33. Rxe4 Rxe4
{Question: Find the strongest move. Look at the forcing moves first. Rxe4! The only move
that keeps the advantage: the engine's second choice is about 8.4 pawns worse. It's a
capture.} 34. Rxe4 Qxe4 35. Bh6 Qg6 36. Bc1 Qb1 37. Kf1 Bf5 38. Ke2 Qe4+ {Turning point:
Spassky's 38.Ke2. The engine preferred Ke1 (about -1.7). After the game move it's about
-3.6. Fischer now has the chance to take over.} 39. Qe3 Qc2+ 40. Qd2 Qb3 41. Qd4 Bd3+
{Spassky resigned.} 0-1
